   291	
   292	# Live differential parity checks the Rust kernel
   293	
   294	The owner asked, on 2026-09-29: “can you add a parity check after your rust
   295	engine, with kaggle envcironments? thanks a lot”. Task 1.1 had replayed only
   296	four recorded official episodes; its limits named “a fresh differential run
   297	against Python” as missing. This Reference records the check that fills it and
   298	what it found. It serves the
   299	[[../decisions/restart-the-port-from-isaiahs-clean-base|restart Decision]],
   300	whose Task 1.1 rules kernel it exercises. Searches for parity, differential,
   301	kaggle_environments, generated traces and malformed input found no existing
   302	concept beyond that Decision and the coverage document.
   303	
   304	## Mechanism
   305	
   306	- **Oracle.** `generate_traces.py` runs `kaggle_environments.make("kaggriculture")`
   307	  itself, only when the installed package is 1.32.7 and the engine file's
   308	  SHA-256 equals the `engine_rs/Cargo.toml` pin. The project lock keeps
   309	  kaggle-environments 1.29.0, which has no Kaggriculture environment. The
   310	  generator runs in `uv run --isolated --no-project --with
   311	  kaggle-environments==1.32.7`, and the project manifest and lock stay unchanged.
   312	- **Format.** Traces use the official `kaggriculture-re-parity-v1` records. They
   313	  add a `rejected` record for steps on which Python's interpreter raises, where
   314	  Kaggle's `env.step` keeps its state. Rust must error without mutating state:
   315	  public and private values and key order, statuses, rewards and completion.
   316	- **Inputs.** The inputs come from seeded random, edge-case, Kaggle built-in and
   317	  mixed-seat policies across default, free-hire (250 actors), rich (market-loop
   318	  escape) and custom configurations. There are also 303 one-command probes;
   319	  scripted actions, including a `null` whole action, are submitted exactly.
   320	- **Comparator.** `replay_parity.rs` compares official, committed-generated and
   321	  `KAGG_PARITY_TRACES` traces using the same code. It reports the first line,
   322	  step, kind and field path, with expected and actual values.
   323	- **Classification.** The sweep names a known class only when the observed
   324	  mismatch has that class's signature: D2 needs Python's unhashable
   325	  `TypeError` and Rust acceptance on that line; D1 needs a Rust recheck, with
   326	  that line's Unicode digits spelled in ASCII, to pass the line. Otherwise the
   327	  divergence is new and the sweep exits 1.
   328	- **Custody.** `engine_rs/fixtures/generated/MANIFEST.json` pins 15 traces
   329	  (665,021 bytes); `check_engine_trim.py` validates it, and `TRIM_MANIFEST.json`
   330	  pins the manifest.
   331	
   332	## Findings
   333	
   334	On this version (commits `33e1428`, `8ca378b`), 8 committed games (3,960
   335	transitions) and a 40-game sweep (21,824 transitions) agree completely. That
   336	includes 180 steps Python rejects and Rust also rejects without state change.
   337	Of the 303 probes, 266 agree. The other 37 divergences fall into two classes, both
   338	on malformed input:
   339	
   340	- **D1:** Python `int()` accepts non-ASCII decimal digit strings (`"٣"`,
   341	  `"３"`); Rust errors on them as unit counts and drops them as market
   342	  quantities.
   343	- **D2:** Python raises `TypeError` when an array or object reaches a dict
   344	  lookup: a unit verb, a PLANT crop (also for missing hands), a PICKUP/PLACE
   345	  item, or a BUY_SEED/BUY_ANIMAL item. Rust treats these as no-ops.
   346	
   347	The vendored kernel bytes are pinned, so nothing was repaired. Seven minimal
   348	repros, one-step games whose first action carries only the divergent field, are
   349	expected-failure fixtures with explicit reasons. Rust asserts the
   350	exact divergence, so a change in either engine fails the test. Full-game
   351	policies exclude D1/D2 inputs by default; with them restored, 8 of 12 games
   352	diverge, all classified D1/D2. The model's grammar emits only ASCII names and
   353	integers, so neither class is reachable from policy actions. An adapter that
   354	accepts external actions would need explicit handling.
   355	
   356	## Verification and limits
   357	
   358	Checks run on this branch are recorded in `ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/1.1b/results.md`:
   359	66/66 engine tests, the trim checker, the Python tests (including live
   360	byte-identical regeneration of the committed set), perturbation tests, and
   361	`just prepare`. Codex verification (`verify-1.1b-r1`) approved with edits; the
   362	fixes (confirmed classification, exact null probe, full-state rollback check,
   363	minimal repros, corrected wording) and their checks are recorded in the same
   364	receipt, with the rerun sweep in `ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/1.1b/verify-r1/`.
   365	Generated games use weak policies (most random seats go
   366	bankrupt); only the four official episodes are competitive worlds. Kaggle
   367	framework behavior outside the interpreter is not modeled. The 40-game, 303-probe
   368	sweep is a bounded sample, and a pod-scale sweep remains open. Reopen D1/D2 if
   369	the adapter must accept untrusted actions or if the pinned engine changes.
