  1248	# Rules Parity Coverage
  1249	
  1250	This document is the system of record for Orbit Wars and Kaggriculture parity
  1251	coverage. Keep it updated whenever the Python reference, fixture generators,
  1252	or Rust rules engines change. The Orbit Wars coverage below remains unchanged.
  1253	
  1254	## Covered By Replay Fixtures
  1255	
  1256	Replay fixtures are generated with `scripts/download_replays.py` and loaded from
  1257	`tests/fixtures/orbit_wars_replays/replay-*.jsonl`.
  1258	
  1259	The parity tests fail by default when required fixtures are missing. Set
  1260	`REQUIRE_PARITY_FIXTURES=0` to skip replay parity, and to skip generation parity
  1261	when generation fixtures are missing.
  1262	
  1263	Replay coverage is required for:
  1264	
  1265	- `75930761`: 2 players, 103 recorded transitions.
  1266	- `75926553`: 4 players, 222 recorded transitions.
  1267	
  1268	The replay parity test checks each transition against the Python reference for:
  1269	
  1270	- step counter
  1271	- per-player Kaggle status/reward, mapped to active, won, or lost. The replay
  1272	  check requires exact status parity once Kaggle marks every player `DONE`.
  1273	  Before that global terminal row, Kaggle can keep eliminated players `ACTIVE`
  1274	  while Rust intentionally reports them as `Lost`, so the test only verifies
  1275	  that Rust has not ended the game early.
  1276	- angular velocity
  1277	- planets: id, owner, position, radius, ships, production
  1278	- initial planets
  1279	- fleets: id, owner, position, angle, source planet, ships
  1280	- next fleet id
  1281	- comet planet ids
  1282	- comet groups: planet ids, full paths, path index
  1283	
  1284	Auxiliary Rust `StepResult` counters for fleets and ships lost in the sun, out
  1285	of bounds, or combat resolution, and for planets and comet planets captured,
  1286	plus neutral capture/undershot counters, are not fields in the Kaggle rows.
  1287	They are covered by focused Rust unit tests rather than replay parity
  1288	assertions.
  1289	
  1290	The current local replay set covers both 2-player and 4-player games, launches,
  1291	production, fleet movement, collisions, captures, and comet movement and expiry.
  1292	Step-limit termination is covered by focused Rust unit tests.
  1293	
  1294	## Covered By Generation Fixtures
  1295	
  1296	Generation fixtures are produced by `scripts/generate_reference_fixtures.py`
  1297	from the installed `kaggle-environments` Orbit Wars implementation and written
  1298	to `tests/fixtures/generation/reference_generation.json`. Rust consumes the
  1299	recorded random call stream and compares generated output.
  1300	
  1301	The generated fixture currently covers:
  1302	
  1303	- planet generation from seed `42`
  1304	- current Python-reference random static and fill phases, including the
  1305	  reference fourfold symmetry ordering
  1306	- full reset for 2-player and 4-player games, including angular velocity,
  1307	  planet generation, initial planets, and current random-group home assignment
  1308	- comet paths at spawn steps `50`, `150`, `250`, `350`, and `450`
  1309	- comet path generation with existing comet ids excluded
  1310	- comet path generation where failed attempts occur before success
  1311	- comet ship sampling
  1312	- step-limit no-op terminal tie status/reward mapping, where all tied players
  1313	  win
  1314	
  1315	## Covered By Unit Tests
  1316	
  1317	Rust unit tests cover focused rules behavior that is hard to isolate from full
  1318	replays:
  1319	
  1320	- geometry helpers
  1321	- Python-compatible inclusive `RandomSource::uniform` behavior, including equal
  1322	  bounds
  1323	- RL discrete-target launch-angle selection for static target cones, dynamic
  1324	  target-hit windows, sun avoidance, static and dynamic blocker arc
  1325	  subtraction, strict static arc caching, full-mask masking for fully blocked
  1326	  static targets, and no-op fallback accounting
  1327	- fleet speed curve
  1328	- launch validation and side effects
  1329	- production order
  1330	- fleet movement and removal
  1331	- fleet collision priority: swept planet/comet collisions before out-of-bounds
  1332	  or sun removal
  1333	- sun, planet, out-of-bounds, and simultaneous swept-pair collisions
  1334	- combat resolution, ties, and reinforcement
  1335	- neutral non-comet planet and comet undershot counters
  1336	- comet spawning before same-step movement
  1337	- comet movement and expiry
  1338	- comet planet/path alignment when only part of a comet group expires
  1339	- terminal score ties where all tied players win
  1340	- invalid owner and planet-ID invariant rejection
  1341	- immediate nonterminal elimination and result cardinality matching the actual
  1342	  player count
  1343	- replay accepted-action filtering with exact planet-ID matching and no numeric
  1344	  ID truncation
  1345	- replay skip-spawn injection that keeps step parity isolated from RNG-backed
  1346	  comet generation
  1347	
  1348	## Known Boundaries
  1349	
  1350	Replay parity intentionally injects comet paths and comet ships from the
  1351	expected observation. It also explicitly injects "skip spawn" on comet spawn
  1352	steps where the replay has no new comet, so replay parity never falls through to
  1353	RNG-backed comet generation. This isolates step parity from random generation.
  1354	Comet generation parity is covered separately by generation fixtures.
  1355	
  1356	The Rust simulator receives typed actions and fails fast on invalid actions.
  1357	Kaggle/Python action parsing is outside the inner simulator API. The replay
  1358	harness mirrors Python's accepted-action filtering only to turn historical
  1359	Kaggle replay actions into typed Rust actions.
  1360	
  1361	Floating-point parity uses close comparisons rather than bit-for-bit equality.
  1362	Discrete ids, owners, ship counts, production, and removals match exactly. Final
  1363	terminal statuses match exactly; on nonterminal rows the harness permits Rust's
  1364	intentional early `Lost` status while requiring the game remain nonterminal.
  1365	
  1366	The Rust state stores planets and initial planets in ID-indexed slots. Parity
  1367	comparison iterates live slots in ID order, which matches generated and fixture
  1368	planet ordering because fixture IDs are unique and contiguous for live planets.
  1369	Manual duplicate planet IDs and IDs at/above `MAX_PLANET_ID` are intentionally
  1370	rejected before parity comparison.
  1371	
  1372	Replay and generation fixtures are ignored by Git because full episodes and
  1373	recorded reference streams can be large. A fresh checkout must run
  1374	`scripts/regenerate_test_fixtures.sh` or restore fixtures from cache before
  1375	running required parity. Use `REQUIRE_PARITY_FIXTURES=0 just rs-test` only when
  1376	intentionally skipping replay parity or missing generation fixtures.
  1377	
  1378	RL-only terminal metrics such as `ships_lost_in_combat_per_game`,
