  1483	### Kaggriculture Live Differential Parity
  1484	
  1485	Task 1.1b answers the owner request "add a parity check after your rust engine,
  1486	with kaggle environments". `scripts/kaggriculture_parity/generate_traces.py`
  1487	runs `kaggle_environments.make("kaggriculture", configuration=...)` with an
  1488	explicit seed and records traces in the official
  1489	`kaggriculture-re-parity-v1` format: complete public and private state, statuses
  1490	and rewards after every step. It refuses to run unless the installed package is
  1491	1.32.7 and `envs/kaggriculture/kaggriculture.py` has exactly the SHA-256 pinned
  1492	in `engine_rs/Cargo.toml`; each header records both. The project lock pins
  1493	kaggle-environments 1.29.0, which has no Kaggriculture environment, so the
  1494	generator runs in an isolated environment and the project lock is unchanged:
  1495	
  1496	```sh
  1497	UV_OFFLINE=1 uv run --isolated --no-project --with kaggle-environments==1.32.7 \
  1498	  python scripts/kaggriculture_parity/generate_traces.py --preset committed \
  1499	  --out engine_rs/fixtures/generated --manifest
  1500	```
  1501	
  1502	The format adds one record type. When Python's interpreter raises on an input,
  1503	Kaggle's `env.step` fails and keeps its state; the generator records a
  1504	`rejected` record (actions and Python exception) and then steps the policy's
  1505	fallback actions. Rust must return an error for the same actions without
  1506	changing any state: public and private trees (values and map key order),
  1507	statuses, rewards and completion are compared before and after. Actions are recorded as submitted (after a
  1508	JSON round trip); Kaggle's action-schema defaulting only adds missing top-level
  1509	`farmer`/`hands`/`market` keys, which the interpreter defaults identically.
  1510	
  1511	Policies are seeded and selectable per seat: `random` (legal-looking unit
  1512	commands for every actor, usually meaningful on the actor's tile, plus 0–4
  1513	market orders and occasional over-limit queues); `edge` (the random policy plus
  1514	HIRE until and past the 241-actor model capacity, 0/1023/negative/float/string/
  1515	boolean/null/array/huge quantities, empty orders and markets, duplicate and
  1516	reversed queues, BUY_LAND past three quadrants, unknown and lower-case verbs,
  1517	malformed orders and unit commands, commands for non-existent hands,
  1518	oversubscribed PLANT, malformed whole actions, and uncaught-`int()` probes);
  1519	Kaggle's built-in `pass`, `random` (seeded through the policy RNG) and `starter`
  1520	agents; and mixed seats. Configuration variants cover the defaults, free hires
  1521	with 12 orders per turn (up to 249 hands, 250 actors), 10,000,000 starting money
  1522	for the edge policy's 10^12-unit seed orders, which reach Python's
  1523	100,000-iteration market-loop escape,
  1524	and a custom set (7 turns per day, capacity 30, weed chance 0.2, shop and
  1525	town-centre intervals, hire multiplier 3, 4 orders and sparse `marketParams`
  1526	overrides, including `hinge` and `log10` curves). Seeds include negative and
  1527	above-`u64` integers. The 303 one-command probes step a two-turn preamble (buy
  1528	wheat, a goose and carrots, hire, pick up), then one probe command: every
  1529	malformed unit and order, 33 quantity spellings for PICKUP, PLACE and the four
  1530	quantity-taking market verbs (BUY_SEED, SELL, BUY_PRODUCT, BUY_ANIMAL),
  1531	unhashable verbs and items, missing-hand commands and malformed whole actions,
  1532	including `null`. Scripted actions are submitted exactly; before the Task 1.1b
  1533	verification fix the `null` whole-action probe (probe 296) was replaced by PASS,
  1534	so the sweep recorded at `8ca378b` did not probe it (full-game policies did).
  1535	
  1536	`engine_rs/tests/replay_parity.rs` uses one comparator for three sources and
  1537	reports the first divergence as line, step, kind and field path with expected
  1538	and actual values. Rust starts from configuration and seed only.
  1539	
  1540	| Source | Traces | Transitions | Result |
  1541	| --- | --- | --- | --- |
  1542	| Official recorded episodes | 4 | 2,876 | agree |
  1543	| Committed generated games | 8 | 3,960 (25 Python rejections) | agree |
  1544	| Committed divergence repros | 7 | 7 (5 Python rejections) | expected failures, asserted exactly |
  1545	| Local sweep, 40 games | 40 | 21,824 (155 Python rejections) | agree |
  1546	| Local sweep, 303 probes | 303 | 1,515 | 266 agree, 37 diverge (D1 12, D2 25) |
  1547	
  1548	The committed games are `random` vs `random`, `edge` vs `edge`, built-in
  1549	`starter` vs built-in `random`, `random` vs `edge`, `edge` free-hire, `edge` vs
  1550	`random` rich, `random` vs built-in `starter` custom, and built-in `pass` vs
  1551	`edge` with seed −123,456; seeds are 11, 22, 33, 44, 55, 66, 2^64+7 and −123,456.
  1552	`engine_rs/fixtures/generated/MANIFEST.json` pins each trace's SHA-256, size,
  1553	policies, policy seed, configuration, counts and any expected divergence. The
  1554	15 files total 665,021 bytes (budget 4,000,000). `scripts/check_engine_trim.py` validates
  1555	that manifest, its exact file inventory, the Cargo engine pin and the budget;
  1556	`TRIM_MANIFEST.json` pins the manifest bytes. Rust tests also fail when a
  1557	committed state value, private key order, action or rejection claim is
  1558	perturbed. A Python test regenerates the committed set from the live engine and
  1559	requires identical bytes; it skips with a message when an isolated 1.32.7
  1560	environment cannot be built offline.
  1561	
  1562	The current recorded sweep ran `uv run python
  1563	scripts/kaggriculture_parity/sweep.py --games 40` at commit `6217868` (after the
  1564	verification fixes) on the owner's Mac (single process, 47 s): base seed
  1565	20,260,929, 12 rotating policy/configuration pairs, 343 traces and 23,339
  1566	transitions, 204 Python rejections (155 in games, 49 in probes). All 40 games
  1567	agree; 37 probe divergences are confirmed D1 12 and D2 25 (every D1 ASCII
  1568	recheck passes), with 0 new. Its summary, including per-policy counts and every
  1569	first divergence, is `ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/1.1b/verify-r1/sweep-summary.json`;
  1570	the first sweep at `8ca378b` (same counts, input-only classification, null probe
  1571	submitted as PASS) is kept at `ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/1.1b/sweep-summary.json`.
  1572	With `--include-known-divergences` (12 games, base seed 777), 8 diverge, all
  1573	confirmed (D1 2, D2 6). Set
  1574	`KAGG_PARITY_TRACES=<absolute dir>` (and optionally `KAGG_PARITY_REPORT`) to
  1575	replay a larger directory with the same comparator.
  1576	
  1577	#### Known Divergences
  1578	
  1579	Both classes concern malformed input only. The vendored kernel bytes are pinned,
  1580	so they are recorded, not repaired. Seven minimal repros are expected-failure
  1581	fixtures with these reasons: one-step games (`episodeSteps` 2) whose first
  1582	seat-0 action carries only the divergent field, so each diverges at line 1,
  1583	step 0; the test fails if any stops diverging exactly there. Full-game policies
  1584	exclude these inputs by default so a game can test the rest of the episode;
  1585	`--include-known-divergences` restores them.
  1586	
  1587	The sweep classifies a divergence from the observed mismatch, not from the
  1588	input alone. D2 requires a `rejected` record whose Python error is
  1589	`TypeError: unhashable type`, an unhashable input on that line and a Rust
  1590	`rust_accepted` result. D1 requires a Python-accepted transition with Unicode
  1591	digit input on the divergent line, and a Rust recheck of the same trace with
  1592	only that line's digits spelled in ASCII (what Python's `int()` read) must pass
  1593	that line. Anything else is reported as a new divergence and the sweep exits 1;
  1594	a trace corrupted at `public.day` on a D1 line stays unclassified.
  1595	
  1596	- **D1, Unicode decimal digits.** Python `int()` accepts non-ASCII decimal digit
  1597	  strings such as `"\u0663"` (Arabic-Indic three) and `"\uff13"` (full-width
  1598	  three). In PICKUP/PLACE counts Rust returns an error where Python acts; in
  1599	  BUY_SEED, SELL, BUY_PRODUCT and BUY_ANIMAL quantities Rust drops the order
  1600	  where Python executes three units. First found at step 13 of the free-hire
  1601	  edge game (`BUY_PRODUCT FERTILIZER "\u0663"`, money 2,159 versus 2,460).
  1602	- **D2, unhashable arrays/objects.** Python raises `TypeError`, so Kaggle's step
  1603	  fails, when an array or object reaches a dict lookup: a unit verb
  1604	  (`[["NORTH"]]`), a PLANT crop (also for a non-existent hand, via the atomic
  1605	  seed check), a PICKUP or PLACE item, or a BUY_SEED or BUY_ANIMAL item. Rust
  1606	  treats these as no-ops and accepts the step.
  1607	
  1608	All ASCII quantity spellings agree, including `" 4 "`, `"+2"`, `"0002"`,
  1609	`"1_0"`, floats, booleans, `null`, arrays, 10^30 and uncaught-`int()` errors
  1610	(`"abc"`, `null`, `[1]` counts), which both engines reject. Our policy's
  1611	grammar emits only ASCII verbs, item names and integers, so neither class is
  1612	reachable from model actions; an adapter accepting external actions would need
  1613	explicit handling. The original failing full-game traces, their first
  1614	divergences and the replay receipt are in
  1615	`ops/rebuild-2026-09-29/1.1b/evidence/first-failing-games/`.
  1616	
  1617	Limits: generated games come from seeded random, edge and built-in policies,
  1618	not strong play, and most random seats go bankrupt; the four official episodes
  1619	remain the only recorded competitive worlds. Framework behavior outside the
  1620	interpreter (timeouts, agent errors, `INVALID` statuses) is not modeled. The
  1621	sweep is a bounded sample: 40 games and 303 probes, not exhaustive input
  1622	coverage. A larger pod sweep remains open.
  1623	# Task 1.1b results — live differential parity against Kaggle's engine
  1624	
  1625	Owner request (2026-09-29): “can you add a parity check after your rust engine,
  1626	with kaggle envcironments? thanks a lot”.
  1627	
  1628	- **Question:** Do Kaggle's own Python engine and the vendored Rust kernel
  1629	  produce the same public state, private state, statuses and rewards? Do they
  1630	  agree on invalid input, including inputs Python rejects?
